{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>that you will do us no harm, just as we have not touched you</b> — he says: that you will do us no harm, just as we have not touched you. You are now blessed of YHWH, and it is not in our power to do you harm. But the time may change because of the violence you do, and you may need to return to our land, and we will requite you.\n\nThe sense of \"we have not touched you\" is that our hearts were not enticed concerning your wife to touch any one of you, as in the expression \"he who touches this man or his wife\" (Genesis 12:17), and \"we have done nothing but good with you\" means to guard all that is yours, by commanding the people to be careful concerning you. \"And we sent you away in peace\" — for even in our jealousy of you we did not take from all the wealth that you made with us, but sent you away with all that is yours in peace.\n\nNow this fear that they felt of him — it does not seem that the king of the Philistines feared lest Isaac come upon him in war. Rather, Abraham had given assurances to him, to his grandchild and to his descendant (Genesis 21:23), and now they said in their hearts: because we violated our covenant with Isaac and sent him away from us, he too will violate his covenant with us and his seed will drive our seed from the land. Therefore they made a new covenant with him, and defended themselves by saying to him that they had not violated the first covenant, for they had done only good with him. This is the meaning of \"let there now be an oath between us\" — that we now come with you in an oath to devote to destruction anyone who breaches the covenant, as in the sense of \"to enter into the covenant of YHWH your Elohim and into His oath\" (Deuteronomy 29:11).\n\nAnd it is possible that Abraham was very great and mighty, for there were in his household three hundred and eighteen sword-wielding men, and he had many allies, and he was also a valiant man whose heart was like a lion's heart, and he pursued four very mighty kings and defeated them. When they saw his success, that it was from Elohim, the king of the Philistines feared lest he conquer his kingdom from him — and this is evident from the war of the four kings. Or perhaps he had further heard the word of HaShem that He had given him the land, and therefore he entered into a covenant with him and adjured him: if you will deal falsely with me and with my descendant — for it would be treachery if he rebelled against the king — and he thought that Abraham might live until his grandson reigned, and the sons would do as the fathers did, for Isaac was as great as his father, and the king feared lest he wage war against him when he drove him from his land."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Genesis 12:17 but writes only the phrase without a verse citation; the verse address is supplied here parenthetically for clarity, consistent with his practice elsewhere of citing phrases by content."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:21:50.255545337Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}